## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design privacy-first OTCA paths that enforce data residency, tenant isolation, and masking, going beyond basic streaming.

## Key Concepts

- OTCA data contracts, regional immutable storage, global masked aggregates
- Per-tenant masking (tokenization, redaction) and tenant-key management
- Data lineage, access controls, and residency verification

## Code Example

```javascript
function maskRecord(record, tenantKey) {
  const masked = { ...record };
  if (masked.email) masked.email = hash(masked.email + tenantKey);
  if (masked.phone) masked.phone = redact(masked.phone);
  // redact other PII fields as needed
  return masked;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test masking correctness and residency under synthetic bursts?
- How do you handle schema evolution for masked fields across regions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical policy-driven data ingestion and per-tenant privacy controls in a real OTCA telemetry path.

## Key Concepts
- Ingestion-time privacy gates with per-tenant configurations
- Metadata retention versus content logging strategies
- Partitioned storage architecture and cross-tenant governance

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: enforce log_content gate at ingestion
if (tenant.privacy_config.log_content) {
  log(fullContent);
} else {
  log(content_preview);
  redact(fullContent);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate that dashboards only display data respecting tenant privacy settings?
- What monitoring would you implement to detect privacy gate failures?
- How would you handle retroactive policy changes for historical data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design versioned schemas and apply per-tenant privacy while keeping simple yet realistic OTCA pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Versioned schemas and forward/backward compatibility
- Per-tenant privacy and access control
- Streaming topology and partitioning
- Data lineage and provenance in multi-tenant systems

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: pick defaults for missing fields based on version
function normalizeEvent(evt) {
  const v = evt.version || 1;
  const defaults = v >= 2 ? { device_firmware: 'unknown', telemetry_version: '1.0.0' } : {};
  return { ...defaults, ...evt, version: v };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test schema evolution with real data volumes?
- How would you enforce per-tenant access controls when using a shared storage?

